cow is blue and young (statements 5 and 6).\n- The mouse is round (statement 11), and since it\'s round, it must chase the cat (statement 16).\n- The mouse eats the bald eagle (statement 10).\n- The mouse doesn\'t chase the cow (statement 9).\n- The cow doesn\'t see the mouse (statement 8).\n- The bald eagle doesn\'t eat the mouse (statement 2).\n- The mouse doesn\'t chase the cow (statement 9).\n- The if-then statements: .\n   - If something eats the mouse, it must be round (statement 12).\n   - If the mouse is red, then it sees the cow (statement 13) → but the mouse\'s color isn\'t stated except being round (but roundness is a shape, not color). Wait, the mouse\'s color isn\'t mentioned except in statement 13, which is a conditional. Since the mouse\'s color isn\'t given, the statement 13\'s antecedent (mouse is red) might be false, so the implication holds trivially. So that doesn\'t give us info about the mouse\'s color.\n\nSo, the key point here is that the mouse must chase the cat because it\'s round (statement 16). .\n\nNow, the question is whether the cat chases itself. Let\'s see if any rule would lead to that. .\n\nLooking at the rules involving chasing:.\n\n- The only direct chases are the bald eagle chasing the cat (statement 1), and the mouse must chase the cat (from statement 16). .\n\nIs there any rule that says if something is chased by another, then it must chase itself? Not that I can see. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the cat\'s chasing itself is possible but not required. Since there\'s no explicit statement that the cat does or does not chase itself, maybe it\'s unknown. However, sometimes in logic puzzles, you have to consider if any rules might indirectly imply it. .\n\nWait, let\'s see statement 14 again: If something chases the cat, it doesn\'t chase the mouse. The mouse is chasing the cat (from earlier), so the mouse can\'t chase the mouse (itself), but that\'s okay because the question is about the cat. .\n\nThe cat\'s actions: The cat eats 