*Facts**: The rabbit visits the dog and lion (facts 13, 14), and the bald eagle visits the rabbit (fact 4).\n   - **Conditionals**:\n     - **Conditional 20**: If something visits the rabbit, the rabbit needs the bald eagle. Since the bald eagle visits the rabbit, the rabbit must need the bald eagle.\n     - **Conditional 21**: If something visits the lion (and the lion needs the dog), that entity must need the dog. Since the rabbit visits the lion, the rabbit needs the dog.\n     - **Conditional 19**: If the rabbit visits the lion and needs the dog, the dog is round. This is satisfied, so the dog is round.\n     - **Conditional 15**: Round \u2192 rough. Thus, the dog is rough.\n     - **Conditional 17**: Rough \u2192 visits the lion. Thus, the dog visits the lion (in addition to the lion visiting the dog).\n\n2. **Implications**:\n   - The rabbit\u2019s needs (bald eagle and dog) are inferred but do not directly imply seeing.\n   - No conditionals explicitly link \"seeing\" to other predicates except **conditional 18**, which requires the dog to need the rabbit and the rabbit to see the bald eagle to trigger the bald eagle seeing the dog. However, there is no evidence the dog needs the rabbit.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - The rabbit\u2019s ability to see the bald eagle is **not addressed by any facts or conditionals**. The problem does not provide information about \"seeing\" between the rabbit and the bald eagle. Thus, the statement\u2019s truth value cannot be determined.\n\n### Final Answer:\n**C) Unknown**  \nThe information given does not provide evidence to confirm or deny whether the rabbit sees the bald eagle.",
