the bald eagle.\n     - The dog needs the cat (statement 11), so the cat must eat the bald eagle.\n   - **Statement 17**: If something eats the bald eagle, it is green.\n     - Since the cat eats the bald eagle (from Statement 14), the cat must be green.\n   - **Statement 15**: If something sees the cat and the cat is green, the cat eats the bear.\n     - The bear sees the cat (statement 5), and the cat is green (from above), so the cat must eat the bear.\n   - **Statement 16**: If something eats the bear **and** the dog, the bear is rough.\n     - The cat eats both the bear (from Statement 15) and the dog (statement 7), so the bear must be rough.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - Through logical deductions, the bear is **forced to be rough** due to the conditions in Statements 14\u201317 and 16. \n   - Therefore, the statement \"The bear is not rough\" is **false**.\n\n**Answer**: B) False",
