itional must hold. \n\nHence, the conclusion stands: **B) False**.\n\nWait, but the user initially thought A (True). Maybe they thought that since the mouse is nice and cold, but another rule prevents it? Let me check again.\n\nWait another angle: The conditional says \"If someone is nice and cold, then they chase the mouse.\" But does the mouse actually satisfy the antecedent? Yes, as established. So the consequent must be true. Therefore, the mouse must chase the mouse. \n\nTherefore, the statement \"The mouse does not chase the mouse\" is false. So B is correct. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B, so the user's initial thought was wrong. \n\nBut perhaps I made a mistake in assuming that \"someone\" can be the mouse itself. Let me check the problem statement again. The problem mentions \"someone\" in the conditionals, but \"someone\" is just a placeholder for any entity. Since the mouse is an entity, it can be that someone. \n\nAlternatively, maybe \"they\" in the conditional refers to the same as \"someone,\" so the subject and object are different. Wait, no, the structure is: If someone (X) is nice and cold, then X chases the mouse (Y). Here, Y is the mouse, which is a specific entity. So unless X is the mouse itself, then X chasing Y (mouse) would mean the mouse (X) chases the mouse (Y), which is the same entity. \n\nYes, so if X is the mouse, then Y is the mouse as well. Hence, the mouse would have to chase itself. \n\nTherefore, the conclusion is correct. \n\nThus, the answer is B) False. \n\nWait, but the user originally thought A. Maybe there's a miscalculation here. Let me try a different approach. \n\nLet me list all entities: bear, tiger, dog, mouse. \n\nCheck for each entity whether they chase the mouse. \n\n- The tiger chases the bear (point 10). \n- The dog does not chase the bear (point3). \n- The mouse does not chase the bear (point5). \n\nNow, the conditional: If someone (any entity) is nice and cold, they chase the mouse. \n\nWho is nice? Bear