acceptable even if it creates an obligation for the lion to visit the bald eagle, but since that\'s not mentioned, maybe we can\'t confirm it. Wait, but in logical reasoning, if the premises lead to a necessary conclusion, even if it\'s not stated, we have to accept it. So if the lion is young, then it must visit the bald eagle. But the problem\'s base facts don\'t mention that, so perhaps that\'s a problem. Maybe the conclusion is that the premises are inconsistent, but that\'s unlikely. Alternatively, perhaps the problem allows us to ignore such contradictions and just proceed with the given info.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I need to look elsewhere. Let\'s get back to the original question: is the bald eagle young?.\n\nSuppose we want to see if the bald eagle is young. Let\'s see what conditions would make that true.\n\nIf the bald eagle is young, then by statement 18, it must visit the bald eagle (itself). But visiting oneself isn\'t mentioned, and it\'s unclear if that\'s possible. Since there\'s no mention of the bald eagle visiting itself, perhaps this is impossible, so the bald eagle cannot be young. Wait, but maybe "visits the bald eagle" could mean visiting another bald eagle, but the context only mentions one bald eagle. So perhaps the bald eagle can\'t visit itself, so being young would require an impossible action, so the bald eagle must not be young. Therefore, the answer would be B) False.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle can visit itself, but that\'s a stretch. Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another path. Let\'s see if the bald eagle can be young through other statements.\n\nLooking at statement 20: If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel is red, then that something is young. Since the squirrel is red, if the bald eagle visits the squirrel, then the bald eagle would be young. But does the bald eagle visit the squirrel?.\n\nFrom the base facts, the bald eagle chases the squirrel (statement 2), but not necessarily vi