hite excludes red, we can't assume that. Therefore, Dave could be both, so the statement \"Dave is not red\" could be false (if he is red) or true (if he isn't). Since we can't determine his age (young or not), which determines his redness, the answer is unknown (C).\n\nBut the user thought it's B (False). Maybe I missed something. Let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps there's another rule that forces Dave to be red. Let's see.\n\nSince Dave is quiet and big (from being quiet \u2192 big), and point14 says quiet \u2192 big, so that's okay.\n\nPoint15: Young and quiet \u2192 red. So if he is young, then he must be red. But if he is not young, then he doesn't have to be red. But is there a rule that makes him young?\n\nWait, he is round (given), which makes him nice. Young things are also nice (point11). But that doesn't mean that all nice things are young. So being nice doesn't imply being young, except via other rules.\n\nAlternatively, since he is big and white (from being nice and big), and point10 says big and white \u2192 round, which he is, so that's okay.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a chain of reasoning I'm missing. Let's think of all Dave's attributes:\n\n- Round (given)\n\n- Nice (from round)\n\n- Quiet (from nice)\n\n- Big (from quiet)\n\n- White (from nice and big)\n\nNow, if he is young:\n\n- Then, since he's young and quiet, he must be red (point15).\n\n- But being red would mean he's big and red \u2192 must be young (point12), which is consistent.\n\n- But he's also white, so he's both red and white.\n\nIf he's not young:\n\n- Then he doesn't have to be red.\n\n- He can be white, big, etc., without being red.\n\nTherefore, there's no contradiction in either case. Thus, we can't determine his color; it's possible either way. Therefore, the statement \"Dave is not red\" could be true or false, so the answer is unknown (C).\n\nHmm, so I think the correct answer is C. But the user thought B (False). Maybe I made a mistake in assuming Dave could be young or not. Let me see if there's a rule that requires him to be young.\n\nWait, Dave's age isn't mentioned except through possible inference. The only mentions of age are Bob is young, Gary is young, and Erin is big and red, so Erin is young (via point12).\n\nIs there any other way to infer Dave's age?\n\nNo, unless there's a rule that makes being quiet and big imply something else. For example, point14 says quiet \u2192 big, but that's already satisfied.\n\nAlternatively, since he's nice and young things are nice (point11), but that doesn't mean he has to be young. So no.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is C) Unknown. But maybe the problem assumes that being white and red can't coexist, so since he's white, he can't be red, making the answer True (A).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a different path.\n\nWait, another angle: If something is big and white, it is round (point10). Dave is big and white, so he's round, which matches. But also, all round things are nice (point8), which he is. So that's consistent.\n\nNow, considering the color: if he were red, then he's big and red \u2192 must be young (point12). If he's young and quiet (from being nice), then he must be red (point15). So that loops back. But being white doesn't conflict with red unless the problem says so.\n\nIf the problem's color attributes are exclusive, then white and red can't coexist, so he can't be red. Therefore, \"Dave is not red\" is true (A).\n\nBut the problem didn't state that colors are exclusive. So this is an assumption.\n\nSince this is a logical reasoning test, perhaps the standard is that an object can have multiple colors unless a rule says otherwise. Therefore, the answer is unknown (C).\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem assumes that \"white\" is the only color for nice and big things, so they can't be another color. In that case, being white would exclude red, making the answer A.\n\nBut without explicit rules about color exclusivity, I think the answer is C.\n\nWait, but let me check the original premises again for any mention of color exclusivity:\n\nThe problem states \"All nice, big things are white.\" This is a universal statement: if something is nice and big, then it is white. It doesn't say anything about other colors. So, technically, an object could be both white and red, unless another rule prohibits it. Since there's no such rule, we can't assume that.\n\nTherefore, Dave could be white and red or just white. Since there's no rule preventing him from being red, but also no rule forcing him to be red (unless he's young), and his youth is unknown, the answer is unknown (C).\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is C, but the user thought B. Maybe I made a mistake. Alternatively, perhaps I missed a rule.\n\nWait, another thought: since Dave is white (from point9), and point12 says big and red \u2192 young. If he were red, then he must be young, which would require him to be red (point15). But since he's already white, perhaps the problem's color rules are such that \"white\" is the only color for nice and big, making red impossible. But without explicit exclusion, it's unclear.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem's color attributes are mutually exclusive in the context. Like, if something is white, it can't be red, because being white is a property that overrides or excludes other colors. But that's an assumption.\n\nIn logic puzzles like this, sometimes you have to assume that attributes like colors are mutually exclusive unless stated otherwise. For example, if something is white, it can't be red. If that's the case, then since Dave is white (from point9), he can't be red, making the statement \"Dave is not red\" true (A).\n\nBut I'm not sure. The problem didn't specify that colors can't overlap. This is a crucial point. Without that info, strictly speaking, it's unknown. But maybe in the context of the problem's rules, since being white is a result of being nice and big, perhaps that's the only color allowed for those a