elp with the tiger\'s color?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger\'s color can be inferred from another rule. Let me see if any rule requires the tiger to be a certain color. The only rules involving color are G and H, which are about being blue, and the bald eagle is red. The tiger\'s color isn\'t mentioned elsewhere.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the tiger cannot be blue because of some other implication. Let\'s see.\n\nWait, another approach: Let\'s see if the tiger can be blue without violating any rules, or if there\'s a rule that would make it impossible.\n\nSuppose the tiger is blue. Then, since it\'s blue, rule G is satisfied (it does visit the bear). Now, is there any other rule that would conflict with it being blue?.\n\nSuppose another animal is blue. For example, if the bear were blue, then by rule G it would have to visit the bear, but visiting oneself? Not sure. But that\'s a different scenario.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if any of the existing animals must be a certain color that would conflict with the tiger being blue.\n\nThe bald eagle is already red, so that\'s fixed. The rabbit must be red because it needs the tiger (from rule E). So the rabbit is red. The bear\'s color isn\'t mentioned. The tiger\'s color is in question.\n\nIs there any rule that could lead us to conclude the tiger\'s color?.\n\nLooking at rule E again: If something needs the tiger, it must be red. So any entity that needs the tiger must be red. The entities that need the tiger are:.\n\n- Bald eagle (because it\'s big, per rule A).\n- Rabbit (same reason).\n- Are there others? The bear doesn\'t need the tiger (fact 5 says it needs the bald eagle). The tiger itself doesn\'t need itself, so only the bald eagle and rabbit need the tiger. Therefore, those two must be red. The bald eagle is already red, so that\'s okay. The rabbit must be red, which is an inferred fact.\n\nNow, the tiger\'s color isn\'t restricted by these. The only color-related rules are for blue, which don\'t have any